{"name":"Meadow Dew","hex":"#e2d4ad","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"meadow","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A field or pasture; a piece of land either intentionally cultivated with grass or (especially) naturally covered with grass, especially one that is intended to be mown for hay or to be grazed.","Low land covered with coarse grass or rank herbage near rivers and in marshy places by the sea."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cultivate with grass in order to produce hay."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/meadow"},{"term":"dew","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any moisture from the atmosphere condensed by cool bodies upon their surfaces.","Moisture in the air that settles on plants, etc in the morning or evening, resulting in drops."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To wet with, or as if with, dew; to moisten.","To deposit dew."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/dew"}],"entries":[{"title":"Meadow","blocks":[{"text":"A meadow is an open habitat or field, vegetated by grasses, herbs, and other non-woody plants. Trees or shrubs may sparsely populate meadows, as long as they maintain an open character. Meadows can occur naturally under favourable conditions but are often artificially created from cleared shrub or woodland for the production of hay, fodder or livestock. Meadow habitats as a group are characterized as semi-natural grasslands, meaning that they are largely composed of species native to the region, with only limited human intervention."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/5/5e/Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg/330px-Blumenwiese_bei_Obermaiselstein05.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Meadow","wikidata":"Q7777019"},{"title":"Dew","blocks":[{"text":"Dew is water in the form of droplets that appears on thin, exposed objects in the morning or evening due to condensation. \nAs the exposed surface cools by radiating its heat, atmospheric moisture condenses at a rate greater than that at which it can evaporate, resulting in the formation of water droplets."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f1/A_drop_of_dew_on_a_clover.jpg/330px-A_drop_of_dew_on_a_clover.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dew","wikidata":"Q41097"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Bergwiese-Kaernten-2008-Thomas_Huntke.jpg/500px-Bergwiese-Kaernten-2008-Thomas_Huntke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7c/Bergwiese-Kaernten-2008-Thomas_Huntke.jpg/500px-Bergwiese-Kaernten-2008-Thomas_Huntke.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Living meadow, Austria","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABergwiese-Kaernten-2008-Thomas_Huntke.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg/120px-Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg/250px-Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f4/Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg/120px-Hay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AHay_Meadow_-_geograph.org.uk_-_513892.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/fe/Okol%C3%AD_Hukliv%C3%A9ho_001.jpg/120px-Okol%C3%AD_Hukliv%C3%A9ho_001.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campa